    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best protein powder for diabetics is a whey isolate or simple plant protein with close to zero sugar and only a few grams of total carbohydrate per serving. Our top pick is Isopure Zero Carb, with about 25 g of protein and essentially no carbohydrate per scoop, per the label. Protein on its own has a small, slow effect on blood sugar for most people with type 2 diabetes, so a protein-only shake is one of the easier foods to fit into a meal plan. The harder questions are about sweeteners, fiber, whether a shake is replacing a meal, and how it interacts with medication. This guide is not medical advice; anyone on insulin, sulfonylureas or with kidney disease should check with their care team first.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Quick Answer: Our Picks Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table puts sugar and certification side by side with protein, because for most people with diabetes those are the columns that decide whether a product belongs in the cupboard. Price tier compares cost per serving at typical retail prices.
          </p>

