        <div id="blood-sugar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Protein Affects Blood Sugar
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carbohydrate is the macronutrient that raises blood glucose most directly. Protein has a much smaller and slower effect. In people with type 2 diabetes, studies going back decades have found that a protein-only meal produces little rise in blood glucose, although it does stimulate insulin release. That is why a protein shake made with water or unsweetened milk is usually one of the easier things to fit into a diabetes meal plan, provided the powder itself is not carrying hidden sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some research suggests protein may even blunt the glucose rise from a carbohydrate meal. In a 2014 study in Diabetologia, Jakubowicz and colleagues gave people with type 2 diabetes 50 g of whey protein before a high-glycemic breakfast and found a substantially lower blood glucose response after the meal than with a control drink, along with a larger early insulin response. That was a small, short study with a large whey dose, so it does not mean everyone should drink whey before meals, but it is a useful signal that protein and carbohydrate together behave differently from carbohydrate alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Type 1 diabetes is more complicated. Large amounts of protein, especially with fat, can raise blood glucose several hours after a meal in people with type 1, and a 2015 review in Diabetes Care by Bell and colleagues discussed adjusting insulin for high-protein, high-fat meals. Anyone with type 1 who starts a daily shake should watch continuous glucose monitor or fingerstick readings for a few days and talk to their diabetes team about whether their dosing needs to change.
          </p>

          <p>
            The American Diabetes Association's Standards of Care do not set a single ideal ratio of protein, carbohydrate and fat for people with diabetes, and emphasize individualized eating plans. For our wider take on how protein fits a general diet, see our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="carbs-and-sugar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sugar, Total Carbs and Hidden Maltodextrin
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read total carbohydrate, not just sugar. The front of a tub may say low sugar or no added sugar while the nutrition panel shows 10 g or more of carbohydrate per serving, often from maltodextrin, a starch-derived carbohydrate that is digested rapidly and raises blood glucose much like sugar. Maltodextrin does not count as sugar on the label, so a powder can honestly claim 1 g of sugar while carrying a meaningful carbohydrate load. Mass gainers are the extreme case, often delivering 50 g or more of carbohydrate per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolates are the easiest way to keep carbohydrate low. Whey protein isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose and fat, which is why products like Isopure Zero Carb and Dymatize ISO100 sit at around 0 to 2 g of carbohydrate per scoop, per their labels. Whey concentrate keeps more lactose, typically a few grams per scoop. Chocolate flavors usually add a gram or two from cocoa. Our guide to the <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> compares the leanest isolates in more detail.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Aim for 5 g of total carbohydrate or less per serving.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For a protein-only powder, that threshold leaves room for flavoring and a little lactose while ruling out maltodextrin-padded formulas. Then count whatever you mix it with: a cup of dairy milk adds about 12 g of carbohydrate, a banana roughly 25 g.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            What you add to the shake usually matters more than the powder. A low-carb scoop blended with a banana, a cup of juice and a spoonful of honey can easily reach 50 g of carbohydrate. Unsweetened almond or soy milk, a handful of berries, or plain water keep the shake close to the powder's own numbers. If you are counting carbohydrates for insulin dosing, count the whole glass, not the scoop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sweeteners: Sucralose, Stevia, Monk Fruit and Erythritol
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every low-sugar protein powder uses a non-nutritive sweetener to taste sweet without sugar. The common ones are sucralose, acesulfame potassium, stevia leaf extract, monk fruit extract and sugar alcohols such as erythritol. The American Diabetes Association's Standards of Care say non-nutritive sweeteners can be an acceptable replacement for sugar-sweetened products when used in moderation, while generally favoring water and unsweetened options overall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Research on whether these sweeteners affect glucose control is mixed. Most trials find that sucralose, stevia and monk fruit do not raise blood glucose directly, but some studies have raised questions about effects on the gut microbiome and insulin sensitivity, and results differ between individuals. Erythritol has drawn particular attention since a 2023 study in Nature Medicine by Witkowski and colleagues reported an association between higher blood erythritol levels and major cardiovascular events. That study was observational and cannot show cause, but people with diabetes already carry higher cardiovascular risk, so some prefer to avoid erythritol while the research develops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sugar alcohols also have practical downsides. Erythritol, maltitol and sorbitol can cause bloating, gas and diarrhea in larger amounts, and maltitol raises blood glucose more than most people expect. If you want to avoid sweeteners entirely, an unflavored powder such as Naked Pea or Isopure Zero Carb Unflavored lets you sweeten the shake yourself with fruit or cinnamon. If you prefer flavored, choose one sweetener you tolerate and stick with it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fiber" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fiber and Why It Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fiber slows how quickly carbohydrate is absorbed, which can soften the rise in blood glucose after a meal, and soluble fiber is associated with modest improvements in cholesterol. The American Diabetes Association advises people with diabetes to eat at least the amount of fiber recommended for the general population, about 14 g per 1,000 calories, which works out to roughly 25 to 35 g a day for most adults. Most Americans fall well short.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein powders are usually not a fiber source. Isolates contain essentially none, and many plant powders only a gram or two. Premier Protein Powder Plus Fiber is the exception on this list, with 7 g of prebiotic soluble fiber per serving per the brand, about a quarter of a typical daily target. That makes it a reasonable pick for someone who struggles to eat enough vegetables, legumes and whole grains, though food sources remain the better route.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adding fiber to any shake is easy. A tablespoon of chia seeds adds about 4 to 5 g, a tablespoon of ground flaxseed about 2 g, and half a cup of raspberries about 4 g with only a few grams of net carbohydrate. Start small if you are not used to much fiber, because a sudden jump can cause bloating, and drink plenty of water alongside it. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">best protein powder for smoothies</a> guide covers blends that pair well with these additions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="meal-replacement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Meal Replacement vs Protein-Only Shakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are two quite different products in the diabetes aisle. Protein-only powders and shakes, like most of our picks, deliver 25 to 30 g of protein with almost no carbohydrate or fat; they are designed to add protein to a meal or serve as a snack. Meal-replacement shakes, including diabetes-specific formulas such as Glucerna, are built to stand in for a meal, with a mix of carbohydrate, fat, fiber and added vitamins and minerals, and usually less protein. Glucerna Original provides about 10 g of protein, 16 g of carbohydrate, 4 g of fiber and 4 g of sugar in 180 calories per 8 fl oz bottle, per retailer labels at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Meal replacements have a real evidence base in diabetes when they are used as part of a structured program. In the DiRECT trial, published by Lean and colleagues in The Lancet in 2018, adults with type 2 diabetes in UK primary care who followed a total diet replacement program of formula shakes for several months, followed by gradual food reintroduction, achieved remission in 46 percent of cases at 12 months, compared with 4 percent in the control group. Critically, that happened with medication withdrawn under medical supervision.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyday use, the choice comes down to what the shake is replacing. If you skip breakfast and want a shake instead, a meal-style formula or a protein powder blended with fiber, fat and a small portion of fruit gives a more balanced result than protein and water alone. If you want to add protein to an existing meal, or have a snack between meals, a protein-only product makes more sense. For weight loss, which matters for many people with type 2 diabetes, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">best protein powder for weight loss</a> guide goes deeper, including protein while using GLP-1 medications.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kidneys-and-meds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Kidney Disease, Medication and Your Care Team
          </h2>

          <p>
            This section is the most important one on the page. Diabetes is the leading cause of chronic kidney disease, and kidney function changes the right protein intake. The American Diabetes Association's Standards of Care recommend that people with diabetes and chronic kidney disease who are not on dialysis keep protein at about 0.8 g per kilogram of body weight per day, the standard adult RDA, rather than higher. People on dialysis often need more. A daily high-protein shake can push intake above target, so anyone with kidney disease should agree a number with their doctor or a renal dietitian first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Medication is the other big variable. People who take insulin or sulfonylureas such as glipizide or glyburide can experience hypoglycemia if they replace a carbohydrate-containing meal with a near-zero-carb protein shake without adjusting their dose. People taking SGLT2 inhibitors such as empagliflozin or dapagliflozin face a rare but serious risk of ketoacidosis, which the FDA has warned about, and very low carbohydrate intake is one of the recognized risk factors. These are not reasons to avoid protein shakes; they are reasons to tell your care team before changing how you eat.
          </p>

          <p>
            GLP-1 medications such as semaglutide and tirzepatide reduce appetite substantially, and people using them sometimes struggle to eat enough protein to preserve muscle while losing weight. A protein shake can help, but nausea is a common side effect, so smaller servings may work better. This guide is general information, not medical advice. Your doctor, diabetes educator or registered dietitian knows your medication, kidney function and goals, and should have the final word. Our explainer on whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> covers quality and contamination.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How We Picked These Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not test these products on our own blood glucose or send them to a lab. Rankings are built on each product's nutrition panel and ingredient list at the time of writing, the brand's stated testing and certification status, typical retail cost per serving and the published research summarized above. Taste and texture comments reflect patterns in buyer reviews. Our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page explains the method.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first filter was carbohydrate: protein-only products had to come in at about 5 g of total carbohydrate or less per serving and no more than 2 g of sugar, which ruled out most gainers and many plant blends. We then ranked on protein dose, preferring 24 to 30 g per serving, followed by fiber, sweetener transparency and certification. We included Glucerna as a meal-replacement reference point despite its lower protein, because it is the product many people with diabetes are first recommended.
          </p>

          <p>
            We favored isolates over concentrates for their lower lactose and carbohydrate, and included one unflavored pea protein for people who are dairy-free or want to avoid sweeteners entirely. Our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein review</a> covers the Plus Fiber powder and the ready-to-drink shakes in more depth, and our main <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> rankings cover the broader market.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading sugar but not total carbohydrate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A powder can list 1 g of sugar and still carry 10 g or more of carbohydrate from maltodextrin, which raises glucose much like sugar. Check the total carbohydrate line on the nutrition panel, aim for about 5 g or less per serving for a protein-only powder, and count the milk or fruit you mix in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Swapping a meal without adjusting medication</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Replacing a carbohydrate-containing meal with a near-zero-carb shake can cause low blood sugar in people on insulin or sulfonylureas. People on SGLT2 inhibitors should also avoid very low carbohydrate intake without guidance. Tell your care team before using shakes as meal replacements so doses can be reviewed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming diabetes-labeled shakes are high protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Diabetes-specific meal shakes are designed for balanced carbohydrate and fat, not protein. Glucerna Original, for example, provides about 10 g of protein per bottle, less than half of a typical protein powder serving. If your goal is protein, compare the protein line rather than the front label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding protein with kidney disease</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Diabetes is the leading cause of chronic kidney disease, and the ADA recommends about 0.8 g of protein per kilogram a day for people with diabetes and kidney disease who are not on dialysis. A daily high-protein shake can push intake higher. Ask about your kidney function before starting one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning a shake into a dessert</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A low-carb scoop blended with banana, juice and honey can reach 50 g of carbohydrate. Build shakes on water, unsweetened almond or soy milk and a handful of berries, and add chia or flax for fiber. The powder is rarely the problem; the add-ins usually are.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

